# Storage, retention, and resilience

- PostgreSQL is authoritative metadata/state; MinIO stores originals, derived artifacts, datasets, checkpoints, and exports.
- Use workspace-prefixed random object keys, server-side encryption, versioning, checksums, least-privilege service accounts, and short-lived signed operations.
- Never store large blobs in PostgreSQL or treat local container filesystems as durable.
- Retention policies are versioned by data category: conversations, documents, extracted text, embeddings, run traces, exports, datasets, checkpoints, logs, audit, and backups.
- On creation, record the effective policy/version; policy changes define whether existing records are migrated.
- Deletion is a durable workflow covering DB rows, object versions, vectors, caches, derived summaries, training eligibility, and replicas.
- Legal hold overrides normal deletion and is itself audited.
- Audit records have separate retention and cannot be deleted by ordinary workspace administrators.
- “No data loss” cannot be guaranteed on one physical server. Do not claim RPO=0 without synchronous independent replication.
- Production requires an independent failure domain: synchronous PostgreSQL standby for true RPO=0, plus off-host WAL archive/PITR.
- MinIO requires replication or immutable off-host versioned backup; local RAID/snapshots alone are not backup.
- Encrypt backups, separate backup credentials, enforce immutability, monitor completion, and test restores on schedule.
- Define and verify RPO/RTO per component; prefer automated failover only after split-brain and restore procedures are tested.
- Schema migrations are backward-compatible, backed up, observable, and reversible where possible; never reset production.
- Keep enough disk headroom for model download, unpacking, training checkpoints, and rollback. Reject jobs before exhausting disk.
- Derived vector indexes can be rebuilt; preserve originals, metadata, version lineage, and job checkpoints first.
- Recovery tests must prove database, object, ACL, model registry, and audit consistency—not merely that containers start.
